        "label": "What's Going On With the Leafs",
        "body": "The Toronto Maple Leafs are not merely rebuilding; they are aggressively reshaping their identity after an 78-point season that ended in the Atlantic Division's eighth spot. The most seismic move of the last 48 hours is <strong>Sergei Bobrovsky's decision to join the Leafs as their new goaltender</strong>, a signing that instantly stabilizes the crease and signals a front-office commitment to winning. Alongside Bobrovsky, the team secured Gavin McKenna with a three-year entry-level contract, injecting youth into the bottom six while the organization weighs whether to trade Morgan Rielly before camp. This offseason is defined by bold decisions: filling holes, managing the cap, and betting on newcomers like Bobrovsky and McKenna to elevate a roster that finished short of the playoffs. <strong>What to watch next is how the front office leverages Bobrovsky's arrival to unlock further roster moves before training camp</strong>, as the Leafs aim to turn this draft-and-free-agency frenzy into a playoff-ready 2026-27 squad.",
